WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.907
PIV certificates aren't issued by
Cloud PKI or by the program's AD CS,

2
00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:08.263
and the migration doesn't
change their issuer.

3
00:00:08.263 --> 00:00:13.576
The Federal PKI's root for the federal
government is the Federal Common Policy CA

4
00:00:13.576 --> 00:00:16.749
G2, and shared service
providers are contracted

5
00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:19.287
to issue the certificates on PIV cards.

6
00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:23.688
What the target PKI has to do
for PIV is trust, not issue.

7
00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:27.607
Distribute the federal root,
because it isn't in the Microsoft,

8
00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:32.219
Mozilla or Apple trust stores by
default: an Intune trusted certificate

9
00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:36.138
profile for managed devices,
Group Policy for domain members.

10
00:00:36.138 --> 00:00:38.773
Keep NTAuth right while the domain exists,

11
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:43.668
because smart card logon with certificates
from a non-Microsoft CA needs that

12
00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:48.863
issuing CA in NTAuth, and a certificate on
every authenticating domain controller.

13
00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:50.951
And upload the chain to Entra ID,

14
00:00:50.951 --> 00:00:54.887
which validates PIV certificates
against its trust store.

15
00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.100
Then lifecycle.

16
00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.637
Cloud PKI and Intune issue, renew and
revoke what they put on managed devices.

17
00:01:01.637 --> 00:01:04.671
Everything else is the
lifecycle platform's job,

18
00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:08.160
and for this program it's
CyberArk Certificate Manager,

19
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.392
now part of Palo Alto Networks, from the
program's existing machine-identity vendor;

20
00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:16.501
the program doesn't add a
second lifecycle vendor.

21
00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:18.587
Two facts shape how it's deployed.

22
00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:23.685
Its CA account is privileged: to enroll
through a Microsoft CA it holds Read,

23
00:01:23.685 --> 00:01:27.630
Issue and Manage Certificates,
and Request Certificates,

24
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.850
so it falls under the ESC7 review.

25
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:35.122
And in the FedRAMP Marketplace dataset as
of October 20, 2026, none of Palo Alto

26
00:01:35.122 --> 00:01:38.335
Networks' authorized
packages is Certificate

27
00:01:38.335 --> 00:01:41.300
Manager or Next-Generation Trust Security.

28
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.962
Until one is, the program
deploys Certificate Manager,

29
00:01:44.962 --> 00:01:48.462
Self-Hosted inside its own
authorization boundary.

30
00:01:48.462 --> 00:01:52.660
That's a program decision, to
revisit when the Marketplace changes.
